1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,720
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,560
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,220
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,380
Today, our topic is writing.

8
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:21,560
One line a day.

9
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:22,150
On paper.

10
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,170
Ben, did you write anything today?

11
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:26,740
Hi Mia.

12
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:28,260
Hi everyone.

13
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:28,830
Yes, Mia.

14
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,640
Three lines, this morning, with my coffee.

15
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:35,480
"Yesterday: fixed the bike, finally.

16
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:38,910
Now: a little nervous about the meeting.

17
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:45,420
Today: call Dad." Three lines, four minutes, every morning.

18
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:46,810
What about you?

19
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:48,300
Did you write your line?

20
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:49,920
Not yet, Ben.

21
00:00:50,589 --> 00:00:52,580
My line is at night, before bed.

22
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:56,720
But I want to tell you about a notebook I found last week.

23
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:59,440
A small one, in a box, from 2019.

24
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:03,560
And on every page, there was one line on the paper.

25
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:04,660
Only one.

26
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:06,500
"Rain all day.

27
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:09,260
Called Mom." "Bad meeting.

28
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:10,180
Ate soup.

29
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,260
Better." "Saw a fox on the street."

30
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:14,940
A fox, Mia?

31
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:16,300
On your street?

32
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:18,530
And one line, every day?

33
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:20,680
I want to hear the whole notebook.

34
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:23,490
Every day, Ben, for a year.

35
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:27,550
And I sat on the floor and read the whole year in twenty minutes.

36
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:29,430
And I cried a little.

37
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,260
Not because it was sad.

38
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:33,080
Because it was mine.

39
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:38,000
A whole year, kept, in three hundred small lines.

40
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:39,960
I had forgotten the fox.

41
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:41,540
The paper had not.

42
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:43,780
"I had forgotten the fox.

43
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,760
The paper had not." Mia, that is our topic today.

44
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:50,399
So here is our plan.

45
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,080
In part one, we talk about our own lines.

46
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:57,200
How we started, and what a line is.

47
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:03,370
In part two, we ask why writing one line helps the body and the head.

48
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:06,640
And I have a true story for that.

49
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:11,100
In part three, we share simple ideas for tonight's line.

50
00:02:11,170 --> 00:02:11,920
Does that sound good?

51
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:13,720
That sounds good, Ben.

52
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:17,910
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

53
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:19,280
You will hear it again.

54
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:24,020
You can listen while you cook, while you walk, or with a pen in your hand.

55
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:25,780
Okay, Ben.

56
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,220
Part one.

57
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,660
You asked me why I started, in 2019.

58
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,240
Do you want the honest answer?

59
00:02:33,049 --> 00:02:34,860
The honest answer, Mia.

60
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:35,410
Always.

61
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:39,060
Our listeners like the honest one better than the nice one.

62
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:44,360
I did not want to, Ben. 2019 was a hard year.

63
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:45,880
My father was sick.

64
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:47,320
Work was heavy.

65
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:51,980
And I carried everything in my head, all day and all night.

66
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:55,740
And a friend said, "Write one line before bed.

67
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:57,260
Just one.

68
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:02,049
What happened, and how you felt." And I said, "I am not a writer."

69
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:08,300
"I am not a writer." Mia, I said the same thing for thirty years.

70
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,440
I hated writing at school.

71
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:11,769
Essays.

72
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:12,920
Red pen.

73
00:03:13,670 --> 00:03:18,060
"Be more clear." So I did not write anything that was not an email.

74
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:19,710
What did your friend say?

75
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,800
She said, "Nobody is a writer for one line, Mia." And she was right.

76
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:28,780
So that night, I wrote one line.

77
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:30,580
"Hospital.

78
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:31,860
Tired.

79
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:36,390
Dad smiled once." Eight words.

80
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:37,850
And I slept.

81
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,080
For the first time in weeks, I slept.

82
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:45,860
The paper held it, so my head did not have to.

83
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:50,900
"The paper held it, so my head did not have to." Mia, that is how it works.

84
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:55,080
And I started three years ago, with three lines every morning.

85
00:03:55,750 --> 00:03:57,020
Not for anyone.

86
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:57,440
For me.

87
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:01,900
And the strange thing is, the day feels different after three lines.

88
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:02,820
Sorted.

89
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:05,560
Like the bed is made, but inside.

90
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:07,500
Sorted, Ben.

91
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:08,760
I like that word.

92
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:12,700
But three lines every morning, for three years.

93
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,260
Did you ever miss a day?

94
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:16,500
What happens on the days you miss?

95
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:18,600
Many days, Mia.

96
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,560
And nothing terrible happens.

97
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:21,820
That is important.

98
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:23,900
The paper does not punish you.

99
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,320
The next morning, I write, "Missed two days.

100
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,720
Felt busy." That is a line.

101
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:32,380
The missed days become the line.

102
00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:35,240
The notebook is not a teacher with a red pen.

103
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:37,000
It is a friend who waits.

104
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:40,640
Like the bed is made, but inside, Ben.

105
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,660
So let me say what a line is, because people imagine something big.

106
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:47,680
A line is not a poem.

107
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:48,980
It is not a story.

108
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:51,420
It is one sentence about the day.

109
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:54,750
"Rain all day." That is a line.

110
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:56,800
"Fight with my sister.

111
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:59,680
Feel bad." That is a line.

112
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:01,560
And the feeling part, Mia?

113
00:05:02,150 --> 00:05:03,840
Because the line has two halves.

114
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:06,020
What happened, and how you felt.

115
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:08,660
The first half is easy.

116
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,620
The second half is the one people skip.

117
00:05:11,430 --> 00:05:13,600
The second half is the medicine, Ben.

118
00:05:14,670 --> 00:05:15,160
"Rain.

119
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:18,470
Called Mom." That is what happened.

120
00:05:19,030 --> 00:05:19,960
But how did I feel?

121
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:21,150
Tired?

122
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:22,180
Warm?

123
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:23,300
Sad?

124
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:26,800
The feeling word turns the list into a line.

125
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,559
And I noticed that I did not have many feeling words at first.

126
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:31,640
Tired.

127
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:32,280
Okay.

128
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:33,520
Bad.

129
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:35,840
Three words for a whole year.

130
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:38,940
Three words for a whole year of feelings, Mia.

131
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,640
Tired, okay, bad.

132
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:44,580
I think most of us have three.

133
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:48,180
Tired, okay, bad, and that is the whole year.

134
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:49,480
And then?

135
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:52,380
And then the notebook taught me more, Ben.

136
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:53,640
Slowly.

137
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:55,140
Heavy.

138
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:56,400
Light.

139
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:57,820
Warm.

140
00:05:58,800 --> 00:05:59,300
Small.

141
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:00,680
Proud.

142
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:02,200
Jealous.

143
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:04,220
Not from a book.

144
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:06,420
From needing a word for that day.

145
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:07,990
The paper made me find the word.

146
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:10,480
And the word made the feeling smaller.

147
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:15,480
"A word is smaller than a worry." Mia, that is exactly it.

148
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:21,580
Let me tell you how my three lines work, because they are not about feelings, mostly.

149
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:23,860
Line one: what happened yesterday.

150
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,129
Line two: what I feel right now.

151
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:30,840
Line three: one thing I want today.

152
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:32,380
Four minutes.

153
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:34,880
And the middle line is the important one.

154
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:36,720
The middle line, Ben?

155
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:37,740
The feeling?

156
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:38,909
Why that one?

157
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:41,700
Because that is the one I would skip, Mia.

158
00:06:42,449 --> 00:06:45,840
"What do I feel right now?" I do not want to write it.

159
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:48,060
And that is why I must.

160
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:52,640
The line I do not want to write is the line that helps.

161
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:55,540
Okay, so that was part one.

162
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:56,240
Our lines.

163
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:57,720
Now, part two.

164
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:00,840
Why does writing one line on paper help?

165
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:05,280
Mia, my true story is about a scientist and some students.

166
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:07,700
Students and a scientist, Ben?

167
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,640
I did not know science cared about notebooks.

168
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:11,740
What did they write?

169
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:13,520
Okay, part two.

170
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:15,440
This is a true story.

171
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:22,460
In 1986, a psychologist named James Pennebaker, at the University of Texas, did a simple experiment.

172
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,460
He asked students to write.

173
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:27,820
Fifteen minutes a day, for four days.

174
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:30,240
One group wrote about their day.

175
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:31,680
What they did.

176
00:07:32,150 --> 00:07:34,500
The bus, the class, the food.

177
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:36,260
And the other group, Ben?

178
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,680
The one that did not write about the bus and the food?

179
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:40,680
What did they write about?

180
00:07:41,430 --> 00:07:43,820
The other group wrote about their feelings, Mia.

181
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:45,360
The hard things.

182
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:47,779
Things they had not told anyone.

183
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:50,560
Fifteen minutes, four days.

184
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:52,900
And then Pennebaker waited.

185
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:54,820
Six months.

186
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:57,020
And he looked at one thing.

187
00:07:57,840 --> 00:07:59,920
How often did the students go to the doctor?

188
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:01,740
The doctor, Ben?

189
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:03,460
Not their grades?

190
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:04,500
Not their mood?

191
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:05,570
The doctor?

192
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:07,940
That is a strange thing to measure.

193
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:10,380
The doctor, Mia.

194
00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:13,500
And the students who wrote about their feelings went to the doctor less.

195
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:15,070
Much less.

196
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:17,480
For six months.

197
00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:23,520
Four days of writing, fifteen minutes each, and the body was healthier for half a year.

198
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:27,260
Pennebaker did this study many times after that, with different people.

199
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:29,639
And the result kept coming back.

200
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:34,700
Four days of writing, and the body was healthier for six months, Ben.

201
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:35,880
Why?

202
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:38,120
What does the paper do to the body?

203
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:42,520
Pennebaker thinks the feeling that stays inside is work, Mia.

204
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:44,330
The body holds it.

205
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:46,500
All day, all night.

206
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:48,190
Holding is work.

207
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:52,400
And when you write it, the feeling goes outside, on paper.

208
00:08:53,330 --> 00:08:54,589
The body can put it down.

209
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:59,300
The feeling on paper is smaller than the feeling in the head.

210
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:05,480
"The feeling on paper is smaller than the feeling in the head." Ben, that is what I found in 2019.

211
00:09:06,130 --> 00:09:06,709
"Hospital.

212
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:07,770
Tired.

213
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:12,500
Dad smiled once." In my head, that day was a storm.

214
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,020
On paper, it was eight words.

215
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:17,020
And the eight words were true.

216
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,080
The storm was also true.

217
00:09:19,740 --> 00:09:21,840
But the eight words were the part I could hold.

218
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:25,380
"The part I could hold." Yes, Mia.

219
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:28,620
The paper does not make the day smaller.

220
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:30,240
It makes it a size you can hold.

221
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:35,340
Can I ask about the days when the feeling is too big for a line?

222
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:35,880
A death.

223
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:37,190
A breakup.

224
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:40,890
On those days, one line feels like an insult.

225
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:43,080
It does, Ben.

226
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:46,070
When my father died, "Dad died.

227
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:49,039
Feel empty" was too small.

228
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:50,810
And on that night, I wrote more.

229
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:52,980
Not because I should.

230
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:54,460
Because it came.

231
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:55,900
Four pages.

232
00:09:55,980 --> 00:09:57,400
I did not plan it.

233
00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:01,740
The line had made a road, and the big feeling used the road.

234
00:10:02,610 --> 00:10:06,990
"The line keeps the door open for the page." Mia, that is beautiful.

235
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:12,240
Three months of one line, and then, on the hard night, the road was there.

236
00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:17,060
If there had been no notebook that night, the feeling would have had nowhere to go.

237
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:19,020
Nowhere to go, Ben.

238
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:24,420
A head without paper is a room where things come in and nothing goes out.

239
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:26,700
Did you ever stop writing?

240
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:27,960
What happened?

241
00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:30,420
Last winter, Mia.

242
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:35,900
I was busy, I was traveling, and the notebook stayed in the bag for five weeks.

243
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:38,520
And I did not notice anything at first.

244
00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:41,640
Then, week three, the cloud came.

245
00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:44,860
Small worries that did not leave.

246
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:45,570
Bad sleep.

247
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:47,380
And I thought it was work.

248
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:49,240
It was not work.

249
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:52,900
It was five weeks of feelings with nowhere to go.

250
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:56,540
Five weeks of feelings with nowhere to go.

251
00:10:57,250 --> 00:10:59,080
And when you opened the notebook again, Ben?

252
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:04,020
Three lines, and the cloud got smaller the same morning, Mia.

253
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:05,350
Not gone.

254
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:06,600
Smaller.

255
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:09,600
The paper is not a diary.

256
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:11,540
It is where the body puts things down.

257
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:14,220
Okay, so that was part two.

258
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:15,980
Why one line helps.

259
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:19,980
We said the feeling on paper is smaller than the feeling in the head.

260
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:23,860
And we said the line keeps the door open for the page.

261
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:26,460
Now, part three.

262
00:11:26,580 --> 00:11:29,060
Simple ideas for tonight's line.

263
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:30,120
Mia, you go first.

264
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:32,280
Okay, part three.

265
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,080
My first idea is about the fear, Ben.

266
00:11:35,620 --> 00:11:38,020
Because the fear stopped me for years.

267
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:44,750
The fear says, "I have nothing to write." And here is the answer, and it is the whole trick.

268
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:48,120
If you have nothing to write, write that.

269
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:49,120
On any paper.

270
00:11:49,769 --> 00:11:50,710
"Nothing to write.

271
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:52,880
Tired." That is a line.

272
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:53,990
"Nothing.

273
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:57,460
Quiet day." That is a line.

274
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:58,430
"Nothing.

275
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:00,600
Quiet day." is a good line, Mia.

276
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:03,780
My first idea is two rules.

277
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,109
Rule one: no good sentences.

278
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:09,120
The line can be ugly.

279
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:12,780
No verb, no capital letter, spelling wrong.

280
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:14,260
Nobody is grading it.

281
00:12:15,300 --> 00:12:17,400
Rule two: nobody reads it.

282
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:21,840
Not my wife, not my mother, not me, for a month.

283
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:24,780
Good is the enemy of one line.

284
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:31,980
"Good is the enemy of one line." Ben, my second idea is for our listeners learning English.

285
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:36,840
Because this is one of the best English exercises, and it costs nothing.

286
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:39,280
One line a day, in English.

287
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:40,600
Ugly English.

288
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:42,220
"Today rain.

289
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:43,360
I tired.

290
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:46,140
Eat soup." That is English.

291
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:48,840
Real English, about your real day.

292
00:12:49,700 --> 00:12:51,580
Real English about a real day, Mia.

293
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:53,640
And here is what happens.

294
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:57,259
After a month, the line gets a little longer.

295
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:01,200
After three months, the line has a verb in the right place.

296
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:03,919
And you did not study.

297
00:13:04,820 --> 00:13:06,439
You did not notice when it changed.

298
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:09,180
That is how it works with everything.

299
00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:14,180
A friend who waits, Ben, and a line that grows by itself.

300
00:13:15,140 --> 00:13:18,120
Can I ask what the English learners should write about?

301
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:19,880
Because "the day" is big.

302
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:21,660
Three things, Mia.

303
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:23,040
What I ate.

304
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:24,830
One person I saw.

305
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:26,540
One feeling.

306
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:28,240
"Rice.

307
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:29,380
My boss.

308
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:34,120
Nervous." That is a line in English, and it is true.

309
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,400
Food, a person, a feeling.

310
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:39,820
Every day has those three.

311
00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:43,320
And after a month, the three words become a sentence.

312
00:13:44,310 --> 00:13:49,680
"I ate rice with my boss, and I was nervous." You did not study that sentence.

313
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:51,060
You lived it.

314
00:13:52,180 --> 00:13:52,870
You lived it.

315
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,360
And one more thing for the learners, Ben.

316
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:57,580
Do not correct the line.

317
00:13:58,100 --> 00:13:58,830
Not that day.

318
00:13:59,540 --> 00:14:01,400
Write it wrong and close the book.

319
00:14:01,870 --> 00:14:04,020
And once a month, read the old lines.

320
00:14:04,580 --> 00:14:07,780
You will see your own mistakes, and you will fix them yourself.

321
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:11,960
My old ugly lines taught me more than any red pen.

322
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:16,500
More than any red pen, Mia.

323
00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:19,580
My second idea is about the middle of the day.

324
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:21,730
I keep a small paper in my pocket at work.

325
00:14:22,330 --> 00:14:29,140
And when something happens, a hard email, a small win, a strange feeling, I write four words on it.

326
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:30,640
"Angry.

327
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:35,620
Email from Tom." And the four words hold it until night.

328
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:39,340
Four words that hold it until night, Ben.

329
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:43,980
My third idea is the evening line, because that is my main one.

330
00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:49,840
Before bed, after the five small things from our evening episode, I write the line.

331
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:51,080
Two parts.

332
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:53,200
What happened, and how I felt.

333
00:14:54,090 --> 00:14:54,840
"Long day.

334
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:56,230
Fixed the printer.

335
00:14:56,820 --> 00:15:01,060
Felt useful." And then I close the book, and the day is kept.

336
00:15:02,120 --> 00:15:07,860
"The day is kept." Mia, and I noticed that the line is kinder than the day.

337
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:10,160
When I write "Fixed the printer.

338
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:13,780
Felt useful," the day was mostly the printer not working.

339
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,240
But the line remembers the fixing.

340
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:18,740
That is Franklin's question again.

341
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:20,480
What went well.

342
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:23,520
The line is kinder than the day.

343
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:25,460
And my morning line, Ben?

344
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:27,140
You do it in the morning.

345
00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:28,260
Is it different?

346
00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:29,920
It is, Mia.

347
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:32,320
At night, you write what happened.

348
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:35,060
In the morning, I write what I want.

349
00:15:36,129 --> 00:15:40,690
"Today: finish the page, call Dad." One line.

350
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,580
It is the first bird from our list episode, on paper.

351
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,160
Two doors, two lines, two minutes.

352
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:50,400
Night keeps the day.

353
00:15:50,980 --> 00:15:52,340
Morning starts the next one.

354
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:56,160
Two doors, two lines, two minutes.

355
00:15:56,860 --> 00:16:00,980
Let me talk about honesty, Ben, because that is the hard part of the line.

356
00:16:01,580 --> 00:16:10,480
It is easy to write "fine day." It is hard to write "I was jealous of Anna today." But the true line is the one that helps.

357
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:15,280
Pennebaker's students wrote the true things, and their bodies got better.

358
00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:18,580
The true line is the one that helps, Mia.

359
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:23,200
And for the people who are afraid of the true line, because someone might read it.

360
00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:24,720
Nobody reads it.

361
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:26,240
That is rule two.

362
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:30,780
And if that is not enough, here is what Pennebaker told his students.

363
00:16:31,540 --> 00:16:33,400
Write it, and then throw it away.

364
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:34,400
Burn it.

365
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:37,600
The paper only needs to exist while you write.

366
00:16:39,370 --> 00:16:44,800
"The paper only needs to exist while you write." Ben, that takes the fear away.

367
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:46,780
And what about the phone?

368
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:49,340
People ask, "Can I do this on my phone?"

369
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:51,360
You can, Mia.

370
00:16:51,660 --> 00:16:54,070
But I think paper is better, for one reason.

371
00:16:54,750 --> 00:16:56,580
The hand is slow.

372
00:16:56,620 --> 00:17:01,120
When I type, the words come as fast as the feeling, and the feeling stays big.

373
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:07,220
When I write by hand, the hand is slow, and the feeling has to slow down to fit the hand.

374
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:09,600
Paper is slow on purpose.

375
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:12,860
Slow on purpose, Ben.

376
00:17:13,320 --> 00:17:15,560
And there is one more thing about paper.

377
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:17,179
The paper is patient.

378
00:17:17,220 --> 00:17:18,180
It does not answer.

379
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:23,560
When I tell a friend a feeling, the friend answers, and now I have two feelings.

380
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:25,820
The paper only listens.

381
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:29,220
The paper only listens, Mia.

382
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:29,520
Yes.

383
00:17:30,140 --> 00:17:36,290
And it never says, "Have you tried this?" It takes the feeling and keeps it, and asks nothing back.

384
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:38,840
That is why the students got better.

385
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:41,240
Not because someone read it.

386
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:44,530
Because the feeling left the body and landed somewhere quiet.

387
00:17:46,220 --> 00:17:47,760
Somewhere quiet.

388
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,180
And the phone has other things on it, Ben.

389
00:17:50,220 --> 00:17:54,220
The line on the phone sits next to messages and news and the loud room.

390
00:17:54,820 --> 00:17:56,940
The line on paper sits next to nothing.

391
00:17:57,540 --> 00:18:00,640
Just the last line, and the empty space for tomorrow.

392
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,900
The notebook is empty every night, waiting.

393
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:11,080
"The notebook is empty every night, waiting." Mia, my third idea is the smallest version.

394
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:13,140
For the nights when you are finished.

395
00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:16,120
No pen, no book, no words.

396
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:18,299
Here is the smallest line.

397
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:19,519
One word.

398
00:18:20,260 --> 00:18:25,800
"Tired." "Good." "Scared." One word on any paper.

399
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:27,010
A receipt.

400
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:28,740
The back of an envelope.

401
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:31,350
That is still showing up to the page.

402
00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:34,680
Showing up to the page, Ben.

403
00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:38,680
And after a month of showing up, the notebook becomes a place.

404
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:40,320
Not a task.

405
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:41,700
A place I go at night.

406
00:18:42,180 --> 00:18:44,260
Like the window in the morning, or the tea.

407
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:47,080
On hard nights, I want to go there.

408
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:49,040
The first week is a chore.

409
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:50,960
The fourth week is a friend.

410
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:53,030
"The first week is a chore.

411
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:57,879
The fourth week is a friend." Mia, one more thing, for parents.

412
00:18:58,480 --> 00:18:59,400
My daughter is seven.

413
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,450
She writes one line every night, in her own notebook, with a drawing.

414
00:19:04,210 --> 00:19:05,020
"Played with Leo.

415
00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:08,520
Happy." And she reads them to us on Sundays.

416
00:19:09,140 --> 00:19:12,690
She has words for her feelings now, because she needed them for the line.

417
00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:16,180
Learning what you feel, at any age, Ben.

418
00:19:16,320 --> 00:19:17,720
I love that.

419
00:19:17,730 --> 00:19:20,860
And I want to say something to the people who think they are not writers.

420
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:23,940
Because I said that, for years.

421
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:25,160
And it kept me from the notebook.

422
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:30,160
And the notebook was the thing that helped me most in the hardest year of my life.

423
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:32,480
You are not a writer, Mia.

424
00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:34,600
Fine.

425
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:37,080
You are a person with a day, and a pen.

426
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:39,680
And that is our message today, I think.

427
00:19:40,440 --> 00:19:43,140
Feelings that stay inside are work for the body.

428
00:19:44,020 --> 00:19:47,510
Writing them puts them outside, where the body can put them down.

429
00:19:48,530 --> 00:19:49,600
One line is enough.

430
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:51,480
Ugly is enough.

431
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:53,600
One word is enough.

432
00:19:54,600 --> 00:19:56,860
It is the cheapest way to keep a life.

433
00:19:57,740 --> 00:19:59,600
The cheapest way to keep a life.

434
00:20:00,610 --> 00:20:05,650
Ben, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

435
00:20:06,870 --> 00:20:07,180
Write.

436
00:20:07,860 --> 00:20:09,300
To put words on paper.

437
00:20:10,540 --> 00:20:11,620
Write one line.

438
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:13,500
Write it ugly.

439
00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:15,360
Line.

440
00:20:15,960 --> 00:20:16,860
One sentence.

441
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:18,580
One line a day.

442
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:21,120
A one-word line.

443
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:22,780
Paper.

444
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:24,240
The paper holds it.

445
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:25,800
Put it on paper.

446
00:20:27,380 --> 00:20:27,720
Feel.

447
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:30,020
To have an emotion inside.

448
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:31,680
How did you feel?

449
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:33,900
Feel tired.

450
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:35,560
Feel useful.

451
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:37,470
Day.

452
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:39,600
From morning to night.

453
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:41,540
One line a day.

454
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:43,420
The day is kept.

455
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:45,320
Write.

456
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:46,580
Line.

457
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:49,060
Paper.

458
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:49,480
Feel.

459
00:20:50,690 --> 00:20:50,960
Day.

460
00:20:51,940 --> 00:20:53,660
Five words, and one page.

461
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:55,560
And one big idea.

462
00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:57,840
Write one line a day.

463
00:20:58,980 --> 00:21:01,139
Now, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?

464
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:02,520
Yes, Ben.

465
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,100
Everyone listening, here is my question.

466
00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:07,340
What is your line for today?

467
00:21:08,200 --> 00:21:08,720
Right now.

468
00:21:09,520 --> 00:21:11,060
What happened, and how you feel.

469
00:21:11,860 --> 00:21:14,320
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

470
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:15,920
Ugly is fine.

471
00:21:16,690 --> 00:21:17,980
"Listened to a podcast.

472
00:21:18,580 --> 00:21:19,040
Tired.

473
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:21,920
Okay." That is a line.

474
00:21:22,820 --> 00:21:25,980
We read every one, and your line becomes our line.

475
00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:28,619
Your line becomes our line.

476
00:21:29,260 --> 00:21:32,740
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

477
00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:36,180
We make a new episode every few days.

478
00:21:36,260 --> 00:21:40,320
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

479
00:21:41,209 --> 00:21:43,589
The full text of this episode is in the description.

480
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:48,340
Read it, and then close it, and write one line of your own.

481
00:21:48,360 --> 00:21:50,160
Thank you for spending this time with us.

482
00:21:50,800 --> 00:21:51,860
Maybe you are in bed.

483
00:21:52,420 --> 00:21:54,340
Maybe there is a pen somewhere near you.

484
00:21:55,140 --> 00:21:55,600
Find it.

485
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:57,260
Find any paper.

486
00:21:57,860 --> 00:21:59,560
Write one line about today.

487
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:00,740
Ugly.

488
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:01,800
True.

489
00:22:02,560 --> 00:22:02,940
Short.

490
00:22:03,709 --> 00:22:05,220
And then close it and sleep.

491
00:22:06,040 --> 00:22:07,160
The day is kept.

492
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:08,740
You are kept.

493
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:10,180
One line.

494
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:11,760
This is Ben.

495
00:22:12,420 --> 00:22:13,320
And this is Mia.

496
00:22:14,140 --> 00:22:15,800
This was Two Cups English.

497
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:17,460
Write one line.

